From e249a02f7df94ff83dfc692e08c386e900502a84 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FinnE145 <finne014@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 16:30:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] observer: never block on bluetoothd; systemd watchdog

dasbus's default D-Bus call timeout is infinite (GLib.MAXINT), and
upstream made its subscribe calls and control point writes synchronously
without one. On 2026-10-04 bluetoothd never answered a StartNotify after
the link dropped, and the observer's main loop froze for two days: the
phone kept connecting but nothing subscribed, and iOS eventually stopped
reconnecting at all.

- Subscribing is now a chain of asynchronous calls, each with a 10 s
  limit, retried every 2 s (up to 60 times) while the attempt is still
  current and the phone connected.
- Control point writes for attributes and app names are asynchronous,
  queued one at a time (bluetoothd rejects a second outstanding write
  with "In Progress"; a reconnect list means dozens at once),
  each with a limit; failures are a one-line warning, not a traceback.
- Actions stay synchronous (so callers see iOS errors) but are limited.
- main() pings systemd's watchdog from the main loop when WATCHDOG_USEC
  is set, so a blocked loop gets the observer restarted.

---
 ancs4linux/observer/device.py      | 106 ++++++++++++++++++++---------
 ancs4linux/observer/device_comm.py |  54 +++++++++++++--
 ancs4linux/observer/main.py        |  26 +++++++
 3 files changed, 147 insertions(+), 39 deletions(-)

